At this point I will move to FieldMapConfigService because this is where the database work happens.


==================================================
3. SECOND OPEN: FieldMapConfigService.java
==================================================

@Service
public class FieldMapConfigService

@Service tells Spring that this class is a service component.

This class contains the database query and ResultSet mapping logic.


QUERY:

private static final String FIELD_MAP_QUERY =
    "SELECT FIELD_MAP_SK, TABLE_PHYSICAL_NAME, FINAL_QUERY, RUN_FREQUENCY "
  + "FROM C167969RPC.om_catalogue_field_map_lkp "
  + "WHERE is_current_flag = 'Y' "
  + "ORDER BY dwh_created_timestamp";

This is the query provided by Mahesh.

We retrieve four fields:

FIELD_MAP_SK
TABLE_PHYSICAL_NAME
FINAL_QUERY
RUN_FREQUENCY


FROM C167969RPC.om_catalogue_field_map_lkp

This is the Oracle schema and table we are reading from.


WHERE is_current_flag = 'Y'

This ensures that we only retrieve the currently active configuration records.


ORDER BY dwh_created_timestamp

This returns the records in a consistent order based on their created timestamp.


==================================================
4. JdbcTemplate
==================================================

@Autowired
private JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;

We use Spring JdbcTemplate to execute the Oracle SQL query.

JdbcTemplate uses the datasource already configured in Schema Browser.

We do not manually create JDBC connections and we do not put database URLs, usernames or passwords inside the service.

JdbcTemplate also handles the connection lifecycle for us.


WHY JdbcTemplate?

For this requirement we already have a direct SQL query and only need to retrieve a defined set of database fields.

JdbcTemplate is a simple way to execute that query and explicitly map each ResultSet row.

It also gives us direct control over the CLOB field FINAL_QUERY.


==================================================
5. MAIN SERVICE METHOD
==================================================

public List<FieldMapConfigResponse> getCurrentFieldMapConfigs() {
    return jdbcTemplate.query(
        FIELD_MAP_QUERY,
        (ResultSet resultSet, int rowNum) -> mapRow(resultSet)
    );
}

When this method is called:

jdbcTemplate.query()

executes FIELD_MAP_QUERY against the configured Oracle datasource.


(ResultSet resultSet, int rowNum) -> mapRow(resultSet)

For each row returned by Oracle, JdbcTemplate provides a ResultSet.

We send that ResultSet to the mapRow() method.

The mapRow() method converts one database row into one FieldMapConfigResponse object.

Flow:

Oracle Row
   ↓
ResultSet
   ↓
mapRow()
   ↓
FieldMapConfigResponse

JdbcTemplate collects all those objects and returns:

List<FieldMapConfigResponse>


==================================================
6. mapRow() METHOD
==================================================

private FieldMapConfigResponse mapRow(ResultSet resultSet)
        throws SQLException {

This method converts one Oracle result row into one Java response object.


FieldMapConfigResponse response =
        new FieldMapConfigResponse();

First we create an empty FieldMapConfigResponse object.


FIELD_MAP_SK:

response.setFieldMapSk(
    resultSet.getObject("FIELD_MAP_SK", BigDecimal.class)
);

We read FIELD_MAP_SK from the database ResultSet.

We use BigDecimal because the Oracle field is numeric.


TABLE_PHYSICAL_NAME:

response.setTablePhysicalName(
    resultSet.getString("TABLE_PHYSICAL_NAME")
);

TABLE_PHYSICAL_NAME is a character field, so we retrieve it using getString().


FINAL_QUERY:

try {
    response.setFinalQuery(readFinalQuery(resultSet));
} catch (Exception exception) {
    throw new SQLException(
        "Unable to read FINAL_QUERY",
        exception
    );
}

FINAL_QUERY can contain a large SQL statement.

It is stored as a CLOB in Oracle.

Instead of assuming it is a normal VARCHAR/String column, we use a separate method called:

readFinalQuery()

to read the complete CLOB value.

If reading FINAL_QUERY fails, we throw an SQLException with a meaningful error message.


RUN_FREQUENCY:

response.setRunFrequency(
    resultSet.getString("RUN_FREQUENCY")
);

RUN_FREQUENCY is retrieved as a String.


return response;

At this point one database row has been completely mapped into one FieldMapConfigResponse object.


==================================================
7. readFinalQuery() METHOD
==================================================

private String readFinalQuery(ResultSet resultSet)
        throws Exception {

This helper method converts the Oracle FINAL_QUERY CLOB into a Java String.


Clob clob = resultSet.getClob("FINAL_QUERY");

First we try to retrieve FINAL_QUERY as a JDBC CLOB.


if (clob == null) {
    return resultSet.getString("FINAL_QUERY");
}

If the driver does not return a CLOB value, we fall back to reading FINAL_QUERY as a normal String.


StringBuilder value = new StringBuilder();

We use StringBuilder to build the complete query text.

This is useful because FINAL_QUERY can be relatively large.


try (Reader reader = clob.getCharacterStream()) {

We open the CLOB as a character stream.

This is try-with-resources, so Java automatically closes the Reader after we finish using it.


char[] buffer = new char[4096];

We create a buffer of 4096 characters.

IMPORTANT:

4096 is only the size of each chunk we read.

It is NOT the maximum size of FINAL_QUERY.


int charsRead;

This stores how many characters were read in the current iteration.


while ((charsRead = reader.read(buffer)) != -1) {

The reader continues reading the CLOB until there is no more data.

-1 means the end of the stream has been reached.


value.append(buffer, 0, charsRead);

Each chunk that is read is appended to StringBuilder.


return value.toString();

Once the complete CLOB has been read, we convert StringBuilder into a normal Java String.

That String becomes FINAL_QUERY in the API response.


At this point I will move to FieldMapConfigResponse because that class defines the JSON response structure.


